# Single source of truth for tool versions. asdf/mise compatible.
#
# Why: Phase 0 of the rendered-manifests migration pins all CI/render
# tools to deterministic versions to prevent helm-template/kustomize-build
# output drift between local developer machines and CI.
#
# CI workflows reference these versions via env vars; `task dev:verify-tools`
# reads this file and confirms installed binaries match. Drift-check steps in
# gitops-validate.yml and docs-lint.yml fail if the workflow env vars and this
# file disagree.
#
# Update workflow: bump the version here, then update every place that pins
# it. There are four classes:
#   - render tools    -> .github/workflows/{gitops-validate,oci-publish}.yml
#   - openknowledge,  -> the knowledge:* vars in Taskfile.yml (version + the
#     lychee              per-platform checksums). NOT the docs-lint.yml env
#                         block — that pins only `task`; these two reach CI
#                         via `task knowledge:install-cli`.
#   - openspec,       -> package.json + package-lock.json (integrity-hashed),
#     markdownlint        installed via `npm ci --ignore-scripts`
#   - task            -> the TASK_VERSION env in docs-lint.yml AND in
#                         gitops-validate.yml (both drift-checked
#                         there against this file) AND the setup-task
#                         `version:` literal in tofu-validate.yml. THREE sites.
# `task dev:verify-pins` asserts the Taskfile vars + package.json against this
# file; run `task dev:verify-tools` to confirm the installed binaries match.
#
# After an openknowledge bump, also run `task knowledge:rules-apply` and
# commit the regenerated AGENTS.md block, and read the block's diff: the
# built-in rule text comes from that binary and lands verbatim in the file
# agents treat as canonical. A bump is the only event that changes it.
# A bump can also change how the block is PRODUCED, not only what it says --
# 0.5.0 -> 0.12.0 moved `rules` under `prompt` and made the bundle config a
# dotfile. So the intake covers behaviour, not only output: read what changed
# between the two versions and record, in the bump commit, what it does to
# network egress, to files written outside --file/--path, and to config
# discovery. `task knowledge:validate` now fails closed on both silent
# failures found so far -- the bundle config no longer being discovered, and
# the telemetry opt-out no longer being honoured. Those assertions are the
# backstop, not the review. The telemetry one earns its place because the
# switch is an env var the CLI reads as ENABLED whenever it does not recognise
# the value, so a rename lands on the permissive side without an error.
